# Deploy Checklist (MANDATORY)

Every deploy to production MUST include ALL of these steps. No exceptions.

## Before commit

1. **Web version bump** — increment the version in ALL three places:
   - `TripPilot/package.json` (`version` field)
   - `TripPilot/src/utils/app-version.ts` (`APP_VERSION`)
   - `TripPilot/public/version.json` (`version` + `bundleUrl`)

2. **Release notes** — add an entry to `TripPilot/src/utils/release-notes.ts`:
   - Add a new object at the TOP of `RELEASE_NOTES` array (newest first)
   - Include `version`, `date`, and `items` in all 3 languages (pt-BR, en, es)
   - Write user-facing copy (what they notice, not technical internals)
   - The test `release-notes.test.tsx` WILL FAIL if the current APP_VERSION has no entry

3. **version.json notes** — update `notes` field with a short summary of changes

## APK version bump (when native changes exist)

If the deploy includes ANY change that affects the Android APK — new Capacitor plugins,
`AndroidManifest.xml` changes, Gradle config changes, native dependencies (`google-services.json`,
Firebase, new permissions), or any reason the user needs a new APK — you MUST ALSO:

4. **Bump native version** in `TripPilot/android/app/build.gradle`:
   - `versionCode` — increment by 1 (integer)
   - `versionName` — increment patch (e.g. `"0.75.0"` → `"0.76.0"`)

5. **Update `latestNativeVersion`** in `TripPilot/public/version.json`:
   - Set to the NEW `versionName` from step 4
   - This is what triggers the "New version available" toast on the installed APK

### How to detect if APK bump is needed

Ask yourself: "Does the user need to install a new APK for this change to work?"
- YES → bump native version (steps 4-5) + full APK rebuild (steps in deploy sequence)
- NO (web-only change) → skip steps 4-5, OTA handles it automatically

Common APK-required changes:
- New Capacitor plugin added/updated (`@capacitor/*`)
- `capacitor.config.ts` changes
- `AndroidManifest.xml` or Gradle changes
- New native permissions
- Firebase / Google Services config changes
- Any file under `TripPilot/android/` modified

## Version numbering

- **Web**: `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH-rc` (e.g. `2.8.1-rc`)
- **Native**: `X.Y.Z` (e.g. `0.75.0`) with integer `versionCode`
- Every deploy gets its OWN version — never ship two different sets of changes under the same version
- If a previous session already bumped to X.Y.Z-rc, bump to X.Y.(Z+1)-rc

## Deploy sequence

### Web deploy (every deploy)
1. `tsc --noEmit` — zero errors
2. `npm run build` — success
3. `npm run test` — no NEW failures (pre-existing are documented)
4. `git add -A && git commit`
5. `git push origin master` — triggers Cloudflare Pages auto-build
6. Verify live: `curl https://trippilot.pages.dev/version.json` shows new version
7. Worker (if changed): `cd worker && npx wrangler deploy`

### APK deploy (when native version was bumped)
8. `npx cap sync android` — sync web assets + plugins to Android project
9. `cd android && ./gradlew assembleRelease` — build unsigned APK
10. Sign APK with the **WSL debug keystore** (cert `c9d309c5`, same as installed):
    `zipalign -f 4 <unsigned>.apk trippilot.apk && apksigner sign --ks /home/julio/.android/debug.keystore --ks-pass pass:android trippilot.apk`
    **CRITICAL**: do NOT use the Windows keystore (`/mnt/c/Users/julio/.android/debug.keystore`) — it has a DIFFERENT cert and Android will refuse the upgrade.
11. Copy to dist: `cp trippilot.apk ../../dist/trippilot.apk`
12. Deploy dist with APK: `npx wrangler pages deploy dist --project-name trippilot`
13. Verify: `curl -sI https://trippilot.pages.dev/trippilot.apk | grep content-length` — size matches new APK

### Post-deploy verification
- Open app on phone — if native version was bumped, "New version available" toast should appear
- If web-only, OTA should auto-apply within ~30s of opening the app
